00:01:37.279 --> 00:01:39.784
Sherose Badruddin: Welcome to
WordPress Accessibility Day 2026.

00:01:39.785 --> 00:01:42.683
My name is Sherose Badruddin, and I'm
a digital accessibility consultant

00:01:42.686 --> 00:01:44.435
at the University of North Carolina.

00:01:44.956 --> 00:01:48.115
Thank you for joining us for The
Renaissance of Achievement for People with

00:01:48.115 --> 00:01:50.701
Disabilities, presented by Ed Summers.

00:01:51.325 --> 00:01:55.134
Ed is a blind software developer
and accessibility executive.

00:01:55.588 --> 00:01:58.691
His personal mission is to empower
people with disabilities to realize their

00:01:58.691 --> 00:02:03.125
full potential in the classroom and the
twenty-first century knowledge economy.

00:02:03.701 --> 00:02:06.289
When his hands aren't on a keyboard,
Ed is typically on the go with

00:02:06.301 --> 00:02:08.442
Loki, his trusted seeing eye dog.

00:02:09.002 --> 00:02:12.798
Please feel free to add your questions
in the Zoom Q&A section, and we'll

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.290
answer them at the end of the session.

00:02:14.619 --> 00:02:16.656
Use the chat to connect
with other attendees.

00:02:18.692 --> 00:02:22.831
Ed Summers: Hello and welcome, and thank
you for that very kind introduction.

00:02:22.916 --> 00:02:27.114
It is absolutely wonderful to
be h- here with you today at

00:02:27.192 --> 00:02:29.221
WordPress Accessibility Day.

00:02:29.632 --> 00:02:30.596
It's terrific.

00:02:30.924 --> 00:02:34.721
Now, before I get started with this
presentation, I wanna thank everyone

00:02:34.802 --> 00:02:39.204
out there listening for taking the
time out of your busy schedule to

00:02:39.204 --> 00:02:44.728
learn more about accessibility,
and most importantly, thank you for

00:02:44.728 --> 00:02:47.249
including accessibility in your craft.

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:53.280
Your individual efforts and your
collective efforts make a difference,

00:02:53.391 --> 00:02:56.335
and we'll touch on that a little
more during this presentation

00:02:58.724 --> 00:03:02.739
This presentation is about the
renaissance of achievement for people with

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:07.408
disabilities, and it's becoming obvious.

00:03:07.436 --> 00:03:10.261
I'm seeing trends in the
disability community that

00:03:10.262 --> 00:03:12.416
are making the renaissance…

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.798
It's becoming clear what's happening.

00:03:14.799 --> 00:03:19.779
So let me just go to the next slide here
and tell you about some of these trends.

00:03:20.626 --> 00:03:24.545
Now, this has been building for a long
time thanks to the, the work that you all

00:03:24.547 --> 00:03:29.574
are doing to make technology accessible
and more inclusion and more opportunities

00:03:29.584 --> 00:03:30.446
for people with disabilities.

00:03:30.485 --> 00:03:35.112
This-- These trends have been building for
quite some time, for years, for decades.

00:03:35.225 --> 00:03:38.562
But just over the last six
months, I'm noticing in the

00:03:38.563 --> 00:03:40.536
disability community these trends.

00:03:40.579 --> 00:03:44.644
Now, as the head of accessibility at
GitHub, you know, it's, it's really

00:03:44.646 --> 00:03:49.434
my job to keep an eye on how people
with disabilities are using technology

00:03:50.093 --> 00:03:53.292
and how technology affects people
with disabilities, and also how

00:03:53.293 --> 00:03:56.285
people with disabilities are starting
to participate in the creation of

00:03:56.285 --> 00:03:57.668
technology, the building of technology.

00:03:57.678 --> 00:04:01.060
Because really what we're trying to
do at GitHub is to empower people with

00:04:01.061 --> 00:04:05.152
disabilities to become builders, to
become innovators, collaborators at both

00:04:05.159 --> 00:04:06.654
the enterprise and open source level.

00:04:08.329 --> 00:04:12.925
I'm seeing fearless experimentation with
technology in the disability community,

00:04:12.925 --> 00:04:17.813
and it's happening-- people who you
would not-- who I would typically not

00:04:17.877 --> 00:04:22.116
think would be fearless experimenters
are starting to really dive in and

00:04:22.116 --> 00:04:25.861
roll their sleeves up and experiment
with tech, and it's super fun to watch.

00:04:26.871 --> 00:04:31.008
We're seeing more productivity and
more innovation across the board.

00:04:32.286 --> 00:04:35.764
And as a result of all this, there's
more leaders with disabilities, people

00:04:35.764 --> 00:04:40.149
with disabilities that are emerging as
leaders with a very strong voice about

00:04:40.449 --> 00:04:43.818
things that they have to share that are
making positive impact in their lives.

00:04:45.101 --> 00:04:51.626
And perhaps most exciting, we're
seeing just a genuine excitement and

00:04:51.626 --> 00:04:56.375
enthusiasm for the future, and it's
accessible technology that's driving that

00:04:58.842 --> 00:05:01.824
Now, I wanna give you a couple examples
of this just to kick this off, and these

00:05:01.824 --> 00:05:06.352
examples are coming from this study
that was published by Anthropic back,

00:05:06.378 --> 00:05:11.359
back in March of, of this year, and it's
called "What 81,000 People Want from AI."

00:05:11.829 --> 00:05:14.712
And there's a link on the screen here
where you can go get it if you need-- if

00:05:14.712 --> 00:05:16.719
you want to take a look at the source.

00:05:17.719 --> 00:05:22.411
I wanna give you a quote from a trades
worker in the United States, and this

00:05:22.411 --> 00:05:24.905
person has a… I'm gonna paraphrase
the quote here, which is on the

00:05:24.905 --> 00:05:30.510
screen, but this person has a reading
disability, and they are saying here

00:05:30.513 --> 00:05:36.485
that AI can read past their reading
disorder, and it is empowering them to

00:05:36.513 --> 00:05:39.080
write code, to start writing code, and
it's something they couldn't do before.

00:05:39.966 --> 00:05:40.791
Got another quote here

00:05:42.982 --> 00:05:46.903
This is from a white-collar worker
in Ukraine, and this person's mute,

00:05:47.605 --> 00:05:53.267
and they said that they, they used
Claude to build a text-to-speech bot.

00:05:53.954 --> 00:05:57.840
And with that little piece of technology
that they built themselves, now they're

00:05:57.848 --> 00:06:01.382
able to have near real-time conversations
with people in their community.

00:06:02.596 --> 00:06:05.261
And it's something-- he says it, or they
say that it's something they dreamed

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:08.656
about and didn't think it was possible,
but now with technology, that's possible.

00:06:08.974 --> 00:06:11.010
And they, and they built it themselves.

00:06:11.011 --> 00:06:11.640
Isn't that amazing?

00:06:12.089 --> 00:06:12.473
All right.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:18.153
Now, I think there are five
drivers of the Renaissance.

00:06:18.951 --> 00:06:19.366
All right?

00:06:19.681 --> 00:06:21.518
And I'll just enumerate
them really fast here.

00:06:21.557 --> 00:06:23.170
We're gonna do a deep
dive on each one of them.

00:06:24.308 --> 00:06:28.894
It starts with driver number one, which
are accessible mainstream AI tools.

00:06:29.867 --> 00:06:33.920
The next one is multimodal lang--
multimodal large language models.

00:06:35.068 --> 00:06:38.810
Number three is AI-native interfaces.

00:06:40.104 --> 00:06:44.328
Number four is the democratization
of software development, and

00:06:44.328 --> 00:06:45.549
number five is open source.

00:06:45.991 --> 00:06:48.280
So before we jump into these

00:06:51.337 --> 00:06:53.351
I need to acknowledge risks of AI.

00:06:54.183 --> 00:06:55.769
These are very real, clear, and present.

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:00.672
The one at the top of the list that, that
perhaps impacts me personally the most

00:07:00.691 --> 00:07:07.296
are-- is right now are the biases, the,
the bias that's built-in to AI models.

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:12.402
And to be clear, that bias comes from
the human, you know, the human output,

00:07:12.402 --> 00:07:13.965
the human content that we've generated.

00:07:14.141 --> 00:07:16.892
It's all that bias is embedded
in that human content.

00:07:17.682 --> 00:07:22.234
And but we do need to remove that bias
from the large language models, okay?

00:07:22.234 --> 00:07:24.706
And there's, there's work in
progress to help make that happen.

00:07:25.771 --> 00:07:28.455
Another one that's just really top of
mind right now, and has been for the

00:07:28.457 --> 00:07:34.587
last few months, as I'm sure you've seen,
are the risks for cybersecurity And of

00:07:34.587 --> 00:07:38.315
course, we all know that there's, there's
a need for more data centers and more

00:07:39.048 --> 00:07:44.585
power generation facilities to drive, you
know, to, to, to satisfy this AI demand.

00:07:44.647 --> 00:07:46.799
And there are environmental
risks that must be managed there.

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:51.047
And then yet another one here is
the potential for rapid change

00:07:51.085 --> 00:07:55.423
in the labor market that could
be basically can be faster than,

00:07:55.792 --> 00:07:57.110
than we can adapt and absorb it.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:07:58.974
Now, I'm an optimist.

00:07:59.101 --> 00:08:01.015
I believe that all these
risks can be managed.

00:08:01.369 --> 00:08:05.475
I know for a fact there are smart
people that are engaged on these risks

00:08:05.509 --> 00:08:11.371
and other risks, and I'm seeing a lot
of really terrific open discussion

00:08:11.427 --> 00:08:14.960
and debate about how to manage
these risks that's happening, you

00:08:14.962 --> 00:08:16.312
know, freely and openly in public.

00:08:16.869 --> 00:08:21.058
And all these are good things, and
I'm optimistic that we are going

00:08:21.058 --> 00:08:22.256
to be able to manage these risks.

00:08:22.257 --> 00:08:23.581
But I'm not gonna focus on them anymore.

00:08:23.582 --> 00:08:28.387
I wanna set them aside for the purposes
of this conversation because I am… I

00:08:28.388 --> 00:08:33.895
wanna focus on the benefits, the benefits
of AI for people with disabilities for

00:08:33.895 --> 00:08:35.186
the next thirty-five minutes or so.

00:08:36.609 --> 00:08:41.232
All right, let's jump to the first driver,
which is accessible mainstream AI tools.

00:08:41.429 --> 00:08:41.751
Now,

00:08:43.866 --> 00:08:47.392
I have on the screen on this slide here, I
ha- I have like what I consider to be the

00:08:47.392 --> 00:08:51.254
major trends in computing that have, that
have affected, you know, humans and users

00:08:51.268 --> 00:08:53.223
over the last fifty year- years or so.

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:56.711
And it starts with
graphical user interfaces.

00:08:56.713 --> 00:09:00.020
Now, graphical user interfaces were
essentially invented back in the '70s.

00:09:00.611 --> 00:09:04.477
It was-- They were adopted, of
course, on Macs during the 1980s,

00:09:04.895 --> 00:09:08.052
and in the early 1990s, it really
went, went mainstream with the, with

00:09:08.052 --> 00:09:11.250
the integration of, you know, GUI,
modern GUI technology into Windows

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:13.712
and other windowing operating systems.

00:09:14.531 --> 00:09:17.505
And throughout that time, people with
disabilities didn't have equal access

00:09:17.507 --> 00:09:19.812
to GUIs, to the windowing systems.

00:09:19.812 --> 00:09:23.621
And, you know, best as I could tell, it
really didn't come online, equal access,

00:09:23.623 --> 00:09:27.021
particularly people who are blind, didn't
come ac- online until maybe the mid-'90s.

00:09:28.187 --> 00:09:30.769
Okay, so we're talking about
from invention to equal access,

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:31.735
we're talking like twenty years.

00:09:32.591 --> 00:09:36.158
Next up, around 1990, the World Wide
Web was invented with web browsers.

00:09:36.158 --> 00:09:39.240
And in the beginning, it was great
because it was primarily text.

00:09:39.445 --> 00:09:43.882
And then we quickly started to
add video and interactive content,

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:46.396
you know, the JavaScript, the
Macromedia, the Adobe Flash.

00:09:47.085 --> 00:09:51.082
And it was, oh goodness, a good
ten or fifteen years later that we

00:09:51.082 --> 00:09:55.725
finally invented the Accessible Rich
Internet Applications or the ARIA API.

00:09:56.341 --> 00:09:59.905
And that became… That
passed as a, as a RFC, as a

00:09:59.906 --> 00:10:02.896
recommendation at the W3C in 2008.

00:10:03.367 --> 00:10:07.708
And, you know, from then on, we're,
we've still been working to adopt ARIA

00:10:07.829 --> 00:10:12.185
and to, to, to include to make all this
rich web-based interaction accessible.

00:10:12.185 --> 00:10:14.252
We've come a long way with
that, but it took decades.

00:10:14.803 --> 00:10:17.549
Next up, in the, in the
2000s, touchscreen devices.

00:10:17.634 --> 00:10:21.007
I can remember when the iPhone first
came out, it was not accessible

00:10:21.007 --> 00:10:21.718
for people with disabilities.

00:10:21.723 --> 00:10:25.121
But with a pretty fast follow-up,
VoiceOver was created, the VoiceOver

00:10:25.121 --> 00:10:29.257
screen reader, which allowed people who
are blind to access those touchscreen

00:10:29.266 --> 00:10:32.146
devices, and of course, there's other
features that have been added for people

00:10:32.148 --> 00:10:34.496
who, who have mobility disabilities.

00:10:36.286 --> 00:10:40.069
Social media in 2010 has really got
big, and I will tell you today, in

00:10:40.074 --> 00:10:43.519
2026, there are still major social
media apps that I as a blind person

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:44.835
cannot access with confidence.

00:10:46.389 --> 00:10:47.736
But generative AI is different.

00:10:48.387 --> 00:10:51.824
Almost from the very beginning, the
developers of, of GitHub Copilot, and

00:10:51.841 --> 00:10:55.534
Microsoft Copilot, and Claude, and
OpenAI, all these other, all these

00:10:55.534 --> 00:10:59.221
other tools, many… Gemini, these
tools have been desi- designed and

00:10:59.225 --> 00:11:00.726
implemented with accessibility in mind.

00:11:00.812 --> 00:11:04.414
And now more than ever, people
with disabilities are getting

00:11:04.534 --> 00:11:09.963
equal access to these tools at the
same time as non-disabled peers.

00:11:10.343 --> 00:11:10.920
It's amazing.

00:11:10.957 --> 00:11:12.114
I will tell you the reason for this.

00:11:12.197 --> 00:11:14.663
A big reason for this is
because of your effort.

00:11:14.783 --> 00:11:20.584
Your collective effort has raised the,
raised accessibility in the public

00:11:20.590 --> 00:11:24.452
consciousness, and the consciousness
of people who build technologies.

00:11:24.506 --> 00:11:28.044
It's amazing, and it's, it's given us
access like we've never had before.

00:11:28.324 --> 00:11:28.665
Okay?

00:11:29.593 --> 00:11:33.254
Let's go into driver number two, which
is multimodal large language models.

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:37.324
Now, these, this is the definition
of modes on the screen here.

00:11:37.641 --> 00:11:43.383
It includes things like text,
images, audio, video, and, and then

00:11:43.384 --> 00:11:48.313
multimodal behaviors or multimodal
features are, is the ability to

00:11:48.314 --> 00:11:52.454
convert seamlessly and fluidly
between one or more of these modes.

00:11:53.468 --> 00:11:57.060
So for example, text to speech and speech
to text, we've been doing that for quite

00:11:57.062 --> 00:12:02.451
some time, as you know, with things like
Siri on the iPhone and the Amazon devices.

00:12:03.884 --> 00:12:06.464
And then just recently, we,
we've large language models.

00:12:06.464 --> 00:12:09.781
In the last few years, large language
models have given us access to convert

00:12:09.781 --> 00:12:14.076
between text to image and image to text,
which is obviously super powerful for

00:12:14.076 --> 00:12:16.019
people who are blind who can't see images.

00:12:16.039 --> 00:12:18.478
We'll touch on that in just
a minute, more in a minute.

00:12:19.624 --> 00:12:22.967
And then really the… I think there's
these last two bullets here are the

00:12:22.973 --> 00:12:26.531
frontier where the really exciting
work is taking place right now, and

00:12:26.531 --> 00:12:30.024
that includes conversion between
text and images, image to text.

00:12:30.035 --> 00:12:31.512
So for example… I'm sorry, I'm sorry.

00:12:31.635 --> 00:12:35.805
Video to text and text to video,
where, where we can get like

00:12:35.819 --> 00:12:38.535
real-time description of video
feeds for people who are blind.

00:12:38.536 --> 00:12:39.116
It's amazing.

00:12:39.855 --> 00:12:42.548
And then there's a lot of people
working on converting sign language

00:12:42.579 --> 00:12:46.456
to, to speech or text and back and
forth, and including Google just had a

00:12:46.456 --> 00:12:49.655
really interesting announcement a few
weeks ago, really nice announcement

00:12:49.672 --> 00:12:52.034
of sign language to text features.

00:12:52.836 --> 00:12:53.083
All right.

00:12:53.083 --> 00:12:56.286
So let's I wanna give you a, a example
that's really personal to me here,

00:12:56.665 --> 00:13:01.593
which is an app called Be My Eyes and a
feature within that app called Be My AI.

00:13:01.627 --> 00:13:06.031
So I have a video here that I'm gonna run,
and let's get started with this video.

00:13:15.358 --> 00:13:19.966
Hans: Be My Eyes is an app for
blind and low vision people where

00:13:19.966 --> 00:13:25.293
you can request a volunteer to see
through your camera on your phone.

00:13:25.694 --> 00:13:28.968
Crystal: It matches people who are
blind or low vision with sighted

00:13:28.971 --> 00:13:32.712
volunteers, and really sky's the
limit for what you can use it for.

00:13:33.016 --> 00:13:38.610
Hans: We have more than half a million
blind and low vision users across

00:13:38.652 --> 00:13:43.149
the globe, and we have 185 different
languages you can choose from.

00:13:43.472 --> 00:13:46.745
Crystal: It really is focused around
how blind and low vision people can

00:13:46.745 --> 00:13:50.658
just be more independent in their world
and have access to these visual tools.

00:13:51.042 --> 00:13:57.180
Hans: Our latest feature with Be My
AI, where you as a blind person take a

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:03.646
photo, and this photo is automatically
uploaded to OpenAI, and you will get a

00:14:03.888 --> 00:14:06.807
detailed description of the photo back.

00:14:07.198 --> 00:14:09.405
BMAI: The image you shared is of a
laptop screen displaying a presentation.

00:14:09.588 --> 00:14:11.652
The slide on the screen has a
dark blue background and contains

00:14:11.654 --> 00:14:13.982
a graph with a title that reads,
"Technology Drives GDP Growth."

00:14:14.627 --> 00:14:20.704
Hans: We have realized that a number
of our users don't use Be My Eyes

00:14:20.755 --> 00:14:25.441
that much, and when we ask them,
they say, "Ah, I don't feel super

00:14:25.459 --> 00:14:27.339
comfortable calling another person."

00:14:27.648 --> 00:14:31.858
So this might be the most important
thing about Be My AI, that now you

00:14:31.861 --> 00:14:37.146
can actually choose if you want
to talk to a person or Be My AI.

00:14:37.624 --> 00:14:39.914
Crystal: Some of my favorite
things to do with it is just get

00:14:40.027 --> 00:14:41.707
descriptions of my surroundings.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:42.923
BMAI: Analyzing picture.

00:14:43.385 --> 00:14:43.773
Please wait.

00:14:44.216 --> 00:14:45.527
The picture you provided is
of a whiteboard with various

00:14:45.527 --> 00:14:46.387
handwritten notes and diagrams.

00:14:46.577 --> 00:14:47.968
The writing appears to be
related to computer programming

00:14:47.968 --> 00:14:48.617
or system architecture.

00:14:48.752 --> 00:14:50.523
The handwriting is a bit messy and
some parts are overlapping- … which

00:14:50.523 --> 00:14:52.204
makes it look like brainstorming or
working through a complex problem.

00:14:52.812 --> 00:14:57.089
Hans: The fact that blind people are
blind all the time, they cannot open

00:14:57.095 --> 00:15:00.613
their eyes and kind of sneak peek
and see what's going on the screen.

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:03.938
For the developers, it
was a new territory.

00:15:04.104 --> 00:15:07.067
It's super important that
you actually develop with the

00:15:07.067 --> 00:15:08.764
people you are developing for.

00:15:09.204 --> 00:15:13.419
The app has definitely improved
from the feedback we have

00:15:13.419 --> 00:15:15.939
gotten from our direct users.

00:15:16.305 --> 00:15:17.348
Crystal: It's empowering.

00:15:17.403 --> 00:15:22.589
I feel like it gives me the chance
to, to do things on my own, to feel

00:15:22.589 --> 00:15:25.939
a sense of independence, to feel a
sense of personal accomplishment.

00:15:26.614 --> 00:15:28.442
Ed Summers: Can you tell me about
the desserts, question mark?

00:15:29.027 --> 00:15:30.386
Mochi and ice cream for $24.

00:15:30.387 --> 00:15:31.488
Chocolate cake for $18.

00:15:31.512 --> 00:15:32.671
Creamy cake for $16.

00:15:32.672 --> 00:15:33.813
BMAI: Cheesecake for $23.

00:15:33.864 --> 00:15:36.345
Hans: It's not just another photo app.

00:15:36.403 --> 00:15:40.533
It is something that actually
changes people's lives.

00:15:41.016 --> 00:15:49.361
It's our belief that Be My Eyes will be
able to help blind people get a job and

00:15:49.412 --> 00:15:52.027
stay in a job that they have right now.

00:15:52.539 --> 00:15:55.324
Crystal: We're still just kind
of at the beginning of, of what's

00:15:55.329 --> 00:15:58.718
possible, and it's gonna be a blast
to see what else they do with it.

00:16:01.819 --> 00:16:05.490
Ed Summers: Okay, driver number
three is AI-native interfaces.

00:16:07.302 --> 00:16:12.231
Now, the model context protocol,
or we call it MCP, is widely used

00:16:12.252 --> 00:16:15.221
to create AI-native interfaces
for large language models.

00:16:16.461 --> 00:16:21.239
And really what MCP servers do, if you
create an MCP server for your system or

00:16:21.748 --> 00:16:26.460
for your website or for the, you know,
the service behind your application, it

00:16:26.462 --> 00:16:31.507
enables large language models to connect
to those systems or, or databases or

00:16:31.507 --> 00:16:35.530
services or whatever they may be, and
on one side, and on the other side, it

00:16:35.530 --> 00:16:38.724
gives the human who's using the large
language model, la- large language model

00:16:38.724 --> 00:16:44.435
the ability to interact with the system
via the MCP server using natural language.

00:16:45.966 --> 00:16:48.721
And it just opens up a world of
possibilities for natural language-based

00:16:48.725 --> 00:16:53.857
queries and, and performing, you know,
completing tasks with natural language

00:16:53.931 --> 00:16:58.204
into these complex systems, where in the
past, we would have to do that through

00:16:58.517 --> 00:17:02.700
an API or some kind of a user interface,
like a graphical user interface.

00:17:03.803 --> 00:17:09.275
Now, there's a ton of benefits for all of
us when it comes to AI-native interfaces.

00:17:09.276 --> 00:17:13.029
It opens up a world of possibilities,
but, but the benefits for people

00:17:13.029 --> 00:17:16.120
with disabilities are just warrant
some brief discussion here.

00:17:16.510 --> 00:17:19.508
In the disability community, there's
something that we call the disability

00:17:19.512 --> 00:17:24.403
tax, and the disability tax refers
to all of the barriers and extra

00:17:24.454 --> 00:17:31.817
friction that we face as we try to use
technology in order to meet our goals.

00:17:32.768 --> 00:17:39.209
And being able to, to access y- you
know, new and existing systems using

00:17:39.309 --> 00:17:44.019
natural language, which is so very
universal, it is… it's just absolutely

00:17:44.019 --> 00:17:47.988
incredible, and it reduces, by a large
degree, it reduces the disability tax.

00:17:50.079 --> 00:17:55.071
And then if you think about, you
know, existing websites and the

00:17:55.072 --> 00:17:56.199
fact that they're designed for…

00:17:56.534 --> 00:17:59.165
Well, I mean, frankly, graphical
user interfaces are, are

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:00.985
designed for people who can see.

00:18:01.522 --> 00:18:05.822
And they have affordances for kind of,
you know, these little visual mechanisms

00:18:05.822 --> 00:18:10.060
that designers use to indicate things
that can be clicked and options,

00:18:10.091 --> 00:18:13.812
operations that can be performed
and, you know, suggest what can be

00:18:13.812 --> 00:18:15.175
done typically with a mouse, frankly.

00:18:15.726 --> 00:18:17.324
And all those affordances are visual.

00:18:17.627 --> 00:18:22.879
And being able to access the, the, the
services and, and, and, and systems

00:18:22.953 --> 00:18:28.245
underneath, you know, a website or an
application with natural language, it

00:18:28.245 --> 00:18:31.608
really just levels the playing field
when it comes to the affordances because

00:18:31.608 --> 00:18:36.377
we can ask using natural language,
"Oh, okay, what can I do here?"

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:39.151
You know, and have a conversation
about that and then go do those things.

00:18:39.703 --> 00:18:42.769
So it just reduces the friction all across
the board for people with disabilities

00:18:44.882 --> 00:18:47.716
All right, let's move on to the
fourth driver, which is the demo- the

00:18:47.918 --> 00:18:50.196
democratization of software development

00:18:54.527 --> 00:18:59.056
Hard to believe, just a year and a half
ago, in March of 2025, a, a developer

00:18:59.190 --> 00:19:04.538
at OpenAI defined this term vibe coding,
and his name was Andrej Karpathy.

00:19:04.818 --> 00:19:06.232
Hopefully I pronounced that correctly.

00:19:06.232 --> 00:19:07.125
Apologies if I didn't.

00:19:07.708 --> 00:19:11.001
But yeah, but vibe coding, that
term kind of caught on immediately

00:19:11.597 --> 00:19:14.831
because it was a, it was a new trend
where people were basically building

00:19:14.838 --> 00:19:16.041
things using natural language.

00:19:17.136 --> 00:19:21.549
And natural language is, is now today
probably the universal programming

00:19:21.550 --> 00:19:26.524
language, and it's really super 'cause you
don't have to learn all the kind of pesky

00:19:26.839 --> 00:19:31.831
semantic details of a, of a JavaScript or
a C++ or, you know, a language like that.

00:19:32.180 --> 00:19:36.214
You can express what you want in
natural language, and it's, it's really

00:19:36.216 --> 00:19:39.946
opened the door for anyone to be able
to turn an idea into working code.

00:19:40.646 --> 00:19:45.840
Now, we all know as professionals in the
field that building highly available,

00:19:45.894 --> 00:19:49.928
you know, fault-tolerant enterprise
class systems requires a new level

00:19:50.037 --> 00:19:55.416
of, you know, an additional level of
expertise beyond expressing what you want.

00:19:55.666 --> 00:19:56.563
Not that it isn't useful there.

00:19:57.029 --> 00:20:01.518
But what I'm seeing that, that's really
super and just, you know, kind of points

00:20:01.518 --> 00:20:08.871
to the democratization term is that vibe
coding is super for building bespoke

00:20:08.913 --> 00:20:13.417
personal tools or solving like, you know,
kind of your own personal access barriers

00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:15.064
or, you know, those kinds of things.

00:20:15.115 --> 00:20:17.114
That's where it, I think it really shines.

00:20:17.678 --> 00:20:20.090
It's like, uh, you know, kind
of programming for the masses.

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:22.146
All right.

00:20:22.772 --> 00:20:24.766
Let's go to number five,
driver number five, and this

00:20:24.766 --> 00:20:26.265
is the last one, is open source

00:20:29.617 --> 00:20:34.664
So due-- because of open source, software
development has become literally the

00:20:34.664 --> 00:20:39.701
world's largest team sport because
everyone can join, and thanks to so

00:20:39.702 --> 00:20:42.851
much of the great work that's been
done at GitHub on accessibility over

00:20:42.853 --> 00:20:46.470
the last four or five years, the
barriers to, to participating have

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:50.783
been greatly, greatly removed, greatly
lowered on the GitHub platform.

00:20:50.785 --> 00:20:51.884
Thanks to my colleagues at GitHub.

00:20:52.815 --> 00:20:58.644
And, you know, open source offers to
us as, as, as builders just a rich

00:20:58.647 --> 00:21:01.850
collection of tools and building blocks
that, that we can start with that,

00:21:01.867 --> 00:21:03.201
that we have a legal right to use.

00:21:03.834 --> 00:21:07.400
And, you know, if, if you look at the
data at your typical application or

00:21:07.401 --> 00:21:09.189
typical website, seventy to ninety…

00:21:09.250 --> 00:21:12.536
somewhere between seventy and ninety
percent of the source code that goes

00:21:12.536 --> 00:21:16.706
into your typical application enterprise,
application or website is open source.

00:21:16.905 --> 00:21:17.414
It's amazing.

00:21:18.576 --> 00:21:22.102
And that's because there are open-source
licenses pr- that provide a legal

00:21:22.113 --> 00:21:27.822
framework for us, for anyone to be able to
share our source code in a way that, that

00:21:27.826 --> 00:21:31.573
others can build upon and, and, and we of
course can build upon the work of others.

00:21:32.017 --> 00:21:33.942
It's a, it's a great
accelerant to the work.

00:21:35.044 --> 00:21:37.856
And then of course, you know, thanks
to, to GitHub and platforms like it,

00:21:38.573 --> 00:21:43.581
it, it provides a, a, just an onl- the
online infrastructure for this globally

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:49.904
distributed collaboration of, of on
technology, on building projects, and

00:21:49.905 --> 00:21:51.353
it's, it's just a beautiful thing.

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:52.945
Okay.

00:21:53.477 --> 00:21:56.974
There is… I've got a video I wanna
show, and it highlights the two

00:21:56.983 --> 00:22:00.374
founders, the two creators, probably
one of the most popular open source

00:22:01.145 --> 00:22:04.676
accessibility projects, assistive
technology products, projects that exist,

00:22:05.025 --> 00:22:11.150
which is NVDA, the Non-Visual Desktop
Access screen reader, which I'm sure

00:22:11.179 --> 00:22:13.373
many of you are at least familiar with.

00:22:13.962 --> 00:22:17.266
And yeah, I'm gonna… we'll,
we'll run this video for a short

00:22:17.269 --> 00:22:18.896
message from the founders of NVDA

00:22:21.447 --> 00:22:23.742
Mick: I was born with a
lot of medical issues.

00:22:23.789 --> 00:22:26.148
Doctors really didn't know
whether I was gonna survive.

00:22:26.148 --> 00:22:30.449
In fact, they made a choice to keep
me alive when I was one day old.

00:22:30.802 --> 00:22:35.120
And so that has been a real
driver that I was given a chance.

00:22:35.451 --> 00:22:41.635
And so therefore, anything that I can do
to make a similar impact for others really

00:22:41.672 --> 00:22:43.981
invigorates me and gives me meaning.

00:22:44.202 --> 00:22:48.158
Access to technology is a make
or break for blind people.

00:22:48.342 --> 00:22:52.610
In order for a blind person to use a
computer, they need special software that

00:22:52.660 --> 00:22:54.855
reads all the text on the computer screen.

00:22:55.065 --> 00:23:00.657
We always used to wonder, "Why do blind
people have to pay so much money when, in

00:23:00.659 --> 00:23:02.538
fact, many blind people are unemployed?"

00:23:02.843 --> 00:23:07.012
That idea remained in my mind,
and I decided to try my hand

00:23:07.012 --> 00:23:09.977
at writing an alternative to
the commercial screen readers.

00:23:10.045 --> 00:23:13.164
Jamie Teh: When Mick approached me with
this idea of writing a completely free

00:23:13.164 --> 00:23:16.773
screen reader, I was intrigued, but a
little bit skeptical, like, look, all

00:23:16.776 --> 00:23:19.440
these companies have been working on
these really expensive products for years.

00:23:19.441 --> 00:23:21.888
What makes us think that we
can write this free thing?

00:23:22.042 --> 00:23:26.724
Mick: In 2006, we started to create
NVDA, which stands for Non-Visual

00:23:26.729 --> 00:23:31.029
Desktop Access, which is a free and
open source screen reader for the

00:23:31.029 --> 00:23:32.657
Microsoft Windows operating system.

00:23:33.046 --> 00:23:35.534
Jamie Teh: We started getting
queries from all around the world.

00:23:35.541 --> 00:23:37.109
Can we translate this into our language?

00:23:37.652 --> 00:23:40.920
And I think that's when we
realized that this had so much

00:23:40.924 --> 00:23:42.736
more potential than we'd imagined.

00:23:43.162 --> 00:23:47.135
A huge part of our philosophy was
how important open source was in

00:23:47.191 --> 00:23:50.929
changing people's lives and everyone
contributing towards a common good.

00:23:51.431 --> 00:23:54.895
We wanted everyone to make a
difference and to empower themselves.

00:23:55.250 --> 00:23:58.240
Part of the development now is actually
done by the community, so there's still

00:23:58.405 --> 00:24:01.591
very much this idea that the community
shapes the future of the product.

00:24:01.854 --> 00:24:05.318
Mick: Knowing that there are blind
people around the world doing

00:24:05.357 --> 00:24:11.201
really cool things, running their
own successful studios, their own

00:24:11.251 --> 00:24:13.571
self-run businesses, it's amazing.

00:24:33.218 --> 00:24:35.701
Ed Summers: All right, let's pick this
back up and summarize where we are here

00:24:38.244 --> 00:24:41.138
There are five drivers of the
renaissance of achievement

00:24:41.139 --> 00:24:42.218
for people with disabilities.

00:24:43.272 --> 00:24:46.189
It starts with accessible
mainstream AI tools.

00:24:46.635 --> 00:24:49.825
People with disabilities are
getting access to these, the

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:53.754
cutting edge of generative AI
technology through these AI tools

00:24:55.998 --> 00:25:00.433
We're on the cusp of really exciting
possibilities with multimodal large

00:25:00.434 --> 00:25:05.959
language models that are converting text
to image, to video, ASL, you name it.

00:25:06.050 --> 00:25:09.004
It's just a very exciting space
that's opening up all kinds of

00:25:09.004 --> 00:25:12.029
possibilities for people with
sensory disabilities in particular.

00:25:12.702 --> 00:25:18.831
The AI native interfaces are opening
up the ability to access all kinds of

00:25:18.847 --> 00:25:23.699
systems, new and old, new and legacy
systems, and avoid the barriers that

00:25:23.699 --> 00:25:28.485
are so frequently inherent within the
user interfaces, like the websites

00:25:28.491 --> 00:25:31.124
and the applications that we must
use, that we traditionally have used--

00:25:31.142 --> 00:25:32.850
had to use to access those systems.

00:25:34.569 --> 00:25:36.704
Software development
is being democratized.

00:25:36.735 --> 00:25:40.983
Natural language has become the
universal programming language, and

00:25:40.983 --> 00:25:44.533
that means that anyone, including
people with disabilities, are finding

00:25:44.534 --> 00:25:48.917
it easier and easier to use the
accessible mainstream AI coding tools

00:25:49.318 --> 00:25:50.995
to solve their own access problems.

00:25:51.999 --> 00:25:57.409
And then when we build something, we
can share it legally as open source and

00:25:57.409 --> 00:26:01.879
collaborate on GitHub on an accessible
GitHub platform and build communities

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:08.009
of like-minded individuals to help
maintain and improve the accessible

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:09.191
solutions that we're creating.

00:26:10.463 --> 00:26:16.502
Now, I wanna leave you with a personal
story, a personal example of how all

00:26:16.502 --> 00:26:19.677
of this recently came together for me
over the last couple of months, and

00:26:19.677 --> 00:26:24.221
it's, it's really… it's this personal
experience that made all this click for

00:26:24.228 --> 00:26:30.344
me, that m- really took the, you know,
what I was observing in the, you know,

00:26:30.387 --> 00:26:33.655
out in the world, in the disability
community, the technology community,

00:26:33.715 --> 00:26:38.521
and after having a kind of a sustained
two or three or four months worth of…

00:26:38.558 --> 00:26:41.699
I was probably about three months
worth of, of tense, sustained personal

00:26:41.742 --> 00:26:45.272
effort, I was like, "Oh, okay. The
game has changed here." All right?

00:26:45.274 --> 00:26:46.780
So let's dig into this personal example.

00:26:49.383 --> 00:26:52.726
Back in March, I attended
the CSUN Assistive Technology

00:26:52.756 --> 00:26:54.411
Conference in Los Angeles.

00:26:54.462 --> 00:26:57.095
I live in Raleigh- Durham on the
East Coast of the United States.

00:26:57.626 --> 00:27:01.946
The CSUN Conference is on the West
Coast out in Anaheim, near Los Angeles.

00:27:01.965 --> 00:27:03.707
Well, it's a week-long conference.

00:27:04.528 --> 00:27:08.818
On the Saturday morning after the
conference, I got up and made my way to

00:27:08.818 --> 00:27:14.830
the Los Angeles International Airport,
got to my plane, sat down in my seat

00:27:14.868 --> 00:27:20.408
on the plane, and some people showed
up beside me, and they refused to sit

00:27:20.409 --> 00:27:22.528
beside me because of my seeing eye dog.

00:27:22.549 --> 00:27:26.374
I have a big German-- a lovely German
shepherd seeing eye dog named Loki, who

00:27:26.374 --> 00:27:29.825
is absolutely wonderful and friendly,
and he was laying there quietly, but

00:27:29.825 --> 00:27:31.489
these people did not wanna sit beside me.

00:27:32.241 --> 00:27:36.578
And it so happens that a few s--
rows behind me, there was a, a

00:27:36.603 --> 00:27:40.272
social media rock star, named Jen
Hamilton, and she started recording

00:27:40.273 --> 00:27:42.094
this interaction and intervened.

00:27:42.444 --> 00:27:46.063
And we have a short clip from
Jen's s- original social media

00:27:46.064 --> 00:27:49.975
post here to share with you.

00:27:50.008 --> 00:27:54.008
[Unintelligible]

00:28:00.900 --> 00:28:02.724
(unknown): This is not right.

00:28:02.819 --> 00:28:03.065
There's something called the Americans

00:28:03.065 --> 00:28:03.141
with disabilities act that allows
us to have our service animal.

00:28:03.141 --> 00:28:03.190
Yeah we have a service animal too.

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:05.464
(unknown): Right I mean- Oh … I'll
be hap- I'll be happy to sit with him.

00:28:06.281 --> 00:28:06.494
Ed Summers: You would?

00:28:06.691 --> 00:28:08.115
Jen: Yeah, I'd be happy to sit with him.

00:28:08.119 --> 00:28:08.308
Thank you.

00:28:08.348 --> 00:28:09.213
Absolutely.

00:28:09.215 --> 00:28:09.686
Ed Summers: Thank you.

00:28:10.340 --> 00:28:11.476
Jen: And are you sure, ma'am?

00:28:11.488 --> 00:28:11.649
Ed Summers: I get

00:28:11.653 --> 00:28:12.775
Jen: to hang out with a dog.

00:28:12.872 --> 00:28:14.953
Oh, I mean, that is an
honor to sit over there.

00:28:14.975 --> 00:28:15.498
Thank you.

00:28:19.187 --> 00:28:20.229
Hi, my name is Jen.

00:28:20.253 --> 00:28:20.825
Can I sit with you?

00:28:20.825 --> 00:28:20.996
Can you, do you

00:28:20.999 --> 00:28:21.847
Ed Summers: mind if I just slide over?

00:28:21.858 --> 00:28:22.626
Jen: Oh, of course.

00:28:22.652 --> 00:28:22.671
Okay.

00:28:22.677 --> 00:28:23.036
Yeah.

00:28:25.086 --> 00:28:27.020
Ed Summers: That was Jen
Hamilton that you heard.

00:28:27.020 --> 00:28:30.714
She recorded that, and she, she
intervened, intervened with a very

00:28:30.801 --> 00:28:34.878
just a wonderful moment of advocacy
for people with disabilities.

00:28:34.983 --> 00:28:38.864
And she recorded that video.

00:28:38.864 --> 00:28:39.437
It's longer than that.

00:28:39.438 --> 00:28:42.047
Actually, it might be a two or
three-minute video, the, the whole thing.

00:28:42.047 --> 00:28:46.229
And, and she published that a few hours
after we landed, and it went viral.

00:28:46.241 --> 00:28:48.669
And I published a follow-up
video, and that video, and that

00:28:48.669 --> 00:28:50.042
went viral on social media.

00:28:50.557 --> 00:28:55.545
And then after a few days, the, the
story hopped over, crossed the chasm

00:28:55.553 --> 00:28:59.773
from social media to mainstream media
and was featured in just a, a wide

00:28:59.773 --> 00:29:02.158
variety of mainstream news outlets.

00:29:02.185 --> 00:29:07.866
And it was just a beautiful moment
where Jen and I really just helped

00:29:07.870 --> 00:29:11.973
a lot of people, literally millions
of people, understand what, what

00:29:12.038 --> 00:29:15.054
effective advocacy can look like
for people with disabilities.

00:29:15.873 --> 00:29:21.704
Now, what the videos did not show
was the conversation that Jen

00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:25.937
and I had all the way from Los
Angeles back to Raleigh-Durham,

00:29:25.937 --> 00:29:26.928
which is a five-hour flight.

00:29:27.775 --> 00:29:30.328
And when Jen approached me,
I had no idea who she was.

00:29:30.331 --> 00:29:34.487
I did not know that she was such an
amazing social media rock star, and by the

00:29:34.489 --> 00:29:36.265
way, a New York Times bestselling author.

00:29:37.144 --> 00:29:45.080
But Jen challenged me to start using
video to, to further my work, my, my

00:29:45.102 --> 00:29:48.743
really my life's work of advocating
for people with disabilities.

00:29:49.624 --> 00:29:55.789
And I accepted her challenge, and
quickly realized that working with video

00:29:55.831 --> 00:30:01.330
as a blind person was gonna be very
difficult due to the limitations that

00:30:01.331 --> 00:30:03.033
existed with the video editing tools.

00:30:03.486 --> 00:30:08.619
Being able to express myself in the vis--
in the, the medium of video as a blind

00:30:08.619 --> 00:30:11.835
person was frankly impossible at the time.

00:30:12.827 --> 00:30:17.623
So of course, being the nerd and the
developer that I am, I turned to GitHub

00:30:17.657 --> 00:30:19.311
Copilot CLI and some other tools.

00:30:19.549 --> 00:30:21.011
And let me walk you through what I did.

00:30:21.283 --> 00:30:24.781
So I… Like I said, I used
the GitHub Copilot CLI.

00:30:25.097 --> 00:30:28.052
That's my, that's my go-to
kind of AI coding tool.

00:30:28.304 --> 00:30:29.434
And, and more than that, actually.

00:30:29.434 --> 00:30:31.484
It's really my go-to tool for
just about everything these days.

00:30:32.106 --> 00:30:35.582
And I started having a conversation
about, well, how can I, how

00:30:35.583 --> 00:30:37.831
can I solve this barrier?

00:30:37.847 --> 00:30:39.083
You know, how can I overcome this barrier?

00:30:39.086 --> 00:30:42.641
How can I create technology that
enables me as a blind person to, to

00:30:42.703 --> 00:30:44.201
really produce videos independently?

00:30:45.163 --> 00:30:48.632
And Copilot and I did many things.

00:30:48.634 --> 00:30:52.940
One, we, we, we built some custom skills,
which I'll talk about in a minute.

00:30:53.539 --> 00:30:56.480
That was skills for Copilot
specifically that work with video.

00:30:57.135 --> 00:31:01.140
And Copilot helped me find this
open source tool called Whisper.

00:31:01.253 --> 00:31:02.534
It's from OpenAI.

00:31:02.554 --> 00:31:05.922
So on the screen here it says
OpenAI/Whisper, and that's

00:31:05.923 --> 00:31:09.413
the name of the, the repo, the
organization and the repo on GitHub.

00:31:09.429 --> 00:31:10.155
That's how that's written.

00:31:10.736 --> 00:31:12.853
And Whisper is a wonderful
open source tool that's…

00:31:13.376 --> 00:31:19.481
that I'm using to, to convert all the
speech in these vi- in videos into, into

00:31:19.482 --> 00:31:24.816
text And to create transcripts of videos
and then, then also closed captions later.

00:31:25.795 --> 00:31:30.912
And then I make extensive use of a,
an amazing open source project called

00:31:30.993 --> 00:31:37.024
FFmpeg, which by the way is really the,
the… It's the project that drives all

00:31:37.288 --> 00:31:42.633
modern video tools and, and video players
that, that you might encou- en-encounter.

00:31:43.346 --> 00:31:44.551
It's really an amazing project.

00:31:44.618 --> 00:31:46.520
If you haven't looked
into FFmpeg, check it out.

00:31:46.799 --> 00:31:51.131
Okay, but using these tools and
these building blocks, I created

00:31:51.518 --> 00:31:55.213
a, what I'm just calling a blind
native video production toolbox.

00:31:56.702 --> 00:32:00.054
And it basically, I… Using this
toolbox, I can basically do these things.

00:32:00.590 --> 00:32:02.754
I can understand the
key moments in videos.

00:32:02.856 --> 00:32:07.335
Now, when I go out and, and I'm
recording, you know, raw video content

00:32:07.335 --> 00:32:12.652
for a video, I might come home with
10, 15, maybe 30 minutes worth of raw

00:32:12.653 --> 00:32:14.160
video, and I gotta understand that.

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:17.884
I need to understand what's happening
visually inside of that, that footage

00:32:17.884 --> 00:32:22.105
so I can pull out, you know, the 60
or 90 seconds that, that, that make

00:32:22.107 --> 00:32:23.908
the story that I really wanna tell.

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:26.257
So I can do that primarily using Skills.

00:32:27.047 --> 00:32:30.950
I can arrange those key moments into
a story using some, you know, some

00:32:31.040 --> 00:32:33.687
FFmpeg-based tools, video editing tools.

00:32:34.224 --> 00:32:37.143
I can reorient videos from
landscape to portrait.

00:32:37.162 --> 00:32:39.109
Sometimes I use a, a GoPro camera.

00:32:39.675 --> 00:32:43.481
When I'm out in the wild, I mounted
a GoPro camera on my, my Seeing Eye

00:32:43.484 --> 00:32:47.880
Dog's harness, and that comes, that
shoots in landscape format, so I have

00:32:47.881 --> 00:32:52.209
to, you know, cut that down to portrait
orientation for, for mobile phones.

00:32:52.859 --> 00:32:57.179
I can add voiceovers and mix that into
the audio, and then I can burn in captions

00:32:57.343 --> 00:32:59.958
before I publish, which of course is
absolutely required for accessibility.

00:33:00.786 --> 00:33:03.954
So these are the things I can do
now, and, you know, some, you know…

00:33:03.985 --> 00:33:05.402
I, I always have human review.

00:33:05.588 --> 00:33:08.978
Sighted human review on the videos
that I create, but I'm at the point

00:33:08.978 --> 00:33:12.910
where, you know, I can create them
independently and more often than not,

00:33:12.998 --> 00:33:17.961
my, my wife, who primarily provides
my sighted review of my videos, gives

00:33:17.961 --> 00:33:19.599
a thumbs up and no further editing.

00:33:20.311 --> 00:33:24.655
So yeah, I wanted to share the first
video that I created with this toolbox

00:33:25.213 --> 00:33:30.317
that I recorded, edited, produced,
and, and published completely 100%

00:33:30.894 --> 00:33:32.314
independently as a blind person.

00:33:42.936 --> 00:33:43.334
That was a fast bike right there.

00:33:43.334 --> 00:33:43.354
Yeah yeah.

00:33:43.354 --> 00:33:43.364
Forward.

00:33:43.364 --> 00:33:45.632
Crossing the street is the most dangerous
thing that Loki and I do together.

00:33:46.178 --> 00:33:50.139
His job is to stop at the down
curbs, and my job is to decide

00:33:50.158 --> 00:33:51.633
when it's safe to cross the street

00:33:55.999 --> 00:33:57.488
This is where Loki really shines.

00:33:57.616 --> 00:34:00.129
