So- All right, let's go

00:36:51.009 --> 00:36:54.196
Sherose Badruddin: Someone asks, "Could
you describe a typical day in the life

00:36:54.223 --> 00:36:56.377
of the accessibility unit at GitHub?"

00:36:56.557 --> 00:37:02.998
And I think it'd be helpful if you touch
on perhaps how developers, um, evaluate

00:37:03.301 --> 00:37:05.290
AI native or AI integrated products.

00:37:07.168 --> 00:37:08.910
Ed Summers: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:37:09.563 --> 00:37:12.054
Typical day in the life, um, well-

00:37:14.106 --> 00:37:19.574
Yeah, so we really started investing
in accessibility in a structured

00:37:19.661 --> 00:37:22.178
way about five years ago at GitHub.

00:37:22.226 --> 00:37:27.389
And, um, about a year into that, the
leadership team created my position,

00:37:27.398 --> 00:37:28.662
which is the head of accessibility.

00:37:29.377 --> 00:37:33.899
Um, you know, to, to basically, um,
accelerate the investment and guide

00:37:33.900 --> 00:37:35.513
the investment from an executive level.

00:37:35.938 --> 00:37:39.228
Uh, I was incredibly blessed
to be recruited into this

00:37:39.234 --> 00:37:42.538
position, and I've been here for
a little over four years now.

00:37:42.596 --> 00:37:46.430
And, um, throughout this, this whole
journey, we spent the first, what, three

00:37:46.455 --> 00:37:51.414
or so years, three or four years, really
focused on those internal systems, um, the

00:37:51.414 --> 00:37:55.923
processes, uh, that are required in order
to integrate accessibility throughout

00:37:56.261 --> 00:37:59.010
not only the product development
process, but throughout the company.

00:37:59.026 --> 00:38:02.719
You know, for, for our events and
our, and our, um, you know, our social

00:38:02.737 --> 00:38:05.746
media, our comms, you know, um, our
workplace, all that kind of stuff.

00:38:06.387 --> 00:38:10.559
Um, and, you know, thanks to
not only our accessibility team

00:38:10.720 --> 00:38:15.167
at GitHub, who are amazing, but
also, um, the employees at GitHub.

00:38:15.394 --> 00:38:18.754
We call them Hubbers, is, is our kind
of nickname for GitHub employees.

00:38:19.399 --> 00:38:21.137
Um, they're amazing.

00:38:21.167 --> 00:38:23.659
I mean, they, they've just been amazing
and, you know, I've been building and

00:38:23.680 --> 00:38:27.398
leading accessibility teams now for
15 or 16 years, about 16 years now.

00:38:28.099 --> 00:38:33.913
And I, I've, in the past,
described accessibility as a

00:38:33.977 --> 00:38:36.302
process of, uh, a Sisyphean task.

00:38:36.347 --> 00:38:40.568
We're referring to Sisyphus, who had
to push the rock up the hill every

00:38:40.569 --> 00:38:44.026
day, and it, at night it rolled back
down the hill, and then he had to do it

00:38:44.026 --> 00:38:46.089
again, same thing again the next day.

00:38:46.091 --> 00:38:51.252
But what has happened at GitHub is, is
I, I don't push the rock up the hill.

00:38:51.289 --> 00:38:54.806
I run downhill trying to keep up
with the rock because it has so much

00:38:54.813 --> 00:38:58.685
momentum, um, because of this wonderful
team of people that we work with here.

00:38:58.687 --> 00:39:03.131
So we're at the point where, you
know, we have accessibility largely

00:39:03.132 --> 00:39:05.104
integrated into our, our processes.

00:39:05.204 --> 00:39:11.435
Um, and, and it's, it's withstanding
the, you know, the, the test of

00:39:11.438 --> 00:39:18.520
time with, you know, just- rocket
fueled, uh, growth of our user base.

00:39:18.876 --> 00:39:21.753
GitHub is up to over 180
million developers now.

00:39:21.753 --> 00:39:23.417
When I came I think it
was about 50 million.

00:39:23.510 --> 00:39:26.064
Um, you know, so, uh,
lots of growth there.

00:39:26.066 --> 00:39:29.097
The growth of AI, which is going…
been incredibly competitive.

00:39:29.097 --> 00:39:31.698
And, and throughout this we've
been able to maintain, um,

00:39:32.072 --> 00:39:36.264
accessibility as a primary, as, as
an engineering fundamental at GitHub.

00:39:36.278 --> 00:39:38.114
So, so it's been really amazing.

00:39:38.155 --> 00:39:43.876
And over the last year or so we've
revamped our strategy, and we've tried

00:39:43.876 --> 00:39:49.347
to build on that success, to build on
that foundation, and to, um… And we've

00:39:49.349 --> 00:39:54.247
added a, a couple additional strategic
priorities for empowering our customers

00:39:54.267 --> 00:39:57.009
that, that are building on GitHub to be
able to meet their accessibility goals.

00:39:57.010 --> 00:40:00.582
So for example, we just recently,
um… Well, not just recently,

00:40:00.584 --> 00:40:04.981
maybe about this time last year,
we, um, released a free and open

00:40:04.983 --> 00:40:07.190
source, um, accessibility scanner.

00:40:07.579 --> 00:40:11.792
Um, you can find that at
github/accessibility-scanner, uh, which

00:40:11.793 --> 00:40:13.505
we are continuing to support and grow.

00:40:13.505 --> 00:40:15.725
And the a- adoption's been really well.

00:40:15.725 --> 00:40:18.939
It's, it's an example of some tools
that we were using internally that we

00:40:18.939 --> 00:40:22.695
refactored and open sourced and, um,
have made available for the community.

00:40:23.535 --> 00:40:27.475
So lots of great growth there, uh,
you know, towards en- en- you know,

00:40:27.511 --> 00:40:30.117
enabling those of you out there who
are building on GitHub to meet your

00:40:30.117 --> 00:40:34.813
accessibility goals and kind of leverage,
you know, um, best practices of, of,

00:40:35.089 --> 00:40:37.032
of automation and AI and, and whatnot.

00:40:37.705 --> 00:40:42.268
Um, and then another, um, initiative
we created is, um, to help improve

00:40:42.268 --> 00:40:43.560
the accessibility of open source.

00:40:44.140 --> 00:40:46.013
The world runs on open source.

00:40:46.033 --> 00:40:51.425
Open source is built on GitHub
by mostly vol- unpaid volunteers.

00:40:51.458 --> 00:40:54.343
I'm sure many of you out there,
uh… Well, WordPress, for example.

00:40:54.430 --> 00:40:55.913
As… Is a great example of that.

00:40:56.741 --> 00:40:57.787
Sherose Badruddin: It sure is.

00:40:57.850 --> 00:40:58.294
Yeah.

